## The idea

Keep every scheduled task in a **priority queue ordered by its due time**, so the next task to run is always at the top. One background **worker thread** loops: look at the top task; if it is due, take it out and run it; if not, sleep until it is due. When someone schedules a new task, we **wake the worker** — the new task may be due earlier than the one it was sleeping for. The task itself runs **outside the lock**, so a slow task never blocks `Schedule`.

## Step by step

1. Store each task as a Handle with its action and the clock time it is due. Put it in a PriorityQueue keyed by (due time, sequence number) — the sequence number keeps tasks with the same due time in the order they were scheduled.
2. Schedule: under the lock, refuse if shut down, add the task, then `Monitor.PulseAll` to wake the worker.
3. Worker: under the lock, peek the top task. Cancelled → throw it away. Due → take it out and leave the lock. Not due → `Monitor.Wait(lock, millisecondsLeft)`, which releases the lock while sleeping and wakes early on a pulse.
4. Run the task outside the lock inside try/catch, so one throwing task cannot kill the worker.
5. Cancel: under the lock, mark the handle cancelled if it has not started yet. The worker skips it when it reaches the top.
6. Shutdown: set a flag and wake the worker. With drainPending the worker keeps running until the queue is empty; without it, it stops at once. A ManualResetEventSlim is set when the worker exits, and AwaitTermination waits on it.

## Worked example

**Input:** At time 0: Schedule(A, 150 ms), Schedule(B, 40 ms), Schedule(C, 90 ms)

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| after the three Schedules | queue by due time: B@40, C@90, A@150; worker wakes on each pulse |
| worker at t=0 | top is B, due in 40 ms → Wait(40) |
| t=40 | B is due → dequeue, run B outside the lock |
| t=40 | top is C, due in 50 ms → Wait(50) |
| t=90 | run C; top is A → Wait(60) |
| t=150 | run A; queue empty → Wait() with no timeout until the next pulse |

**Result:** B, C, A — deadline order, not scheduling order

## Cost

Schedule is O(log n) (heap insert). Each run is O(log n) (heap remove). Cancel is O(1) because a cancelled task stays in the heap until it reaches the top — memory is O(scheduled tasks, including cancelled ones not yet reached).

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// A one-shot delay scheduler: run each action once, after its delay, in deadline order.
public sealed class DelayScheduler : IDisposable {

    // What Schedule returns. The caller can cancel through it.
    public sealed class Handle {
        private readonly DelayScheduler _owner;
        internal readonly Action Work;
        internal readonly long DueMs;      // clock time at which it should run
        internal bool Cancelled;           // these two are only touched under the scheduler's lock
        internal bool Started;

        internal Handle(DelayScheduler owner, Action work, long dueMs) {
            _owner = owner;
            Work = work;
            DueMs = dueMs;
        }

        // true if this call cancelled it; false if it already ran, started, or was cancelled.
        public bool Cancel() {
            return _owner.TryCancel(this);
        }
    }

    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private readonly Func<long> _clock;                       // milliseconds; injectable for tests
    // Smallest (due time, sequence) comes out first.
    private readonly PriorityQueue<Handle, (long Due, long Seq)> _queue = new PriorityQueue<Handle, (long Due, long Seq)>();
    private readonly Thread _worker;
    private readonly ManualResetEventSlim _terminated = new ManualResetEventSlim(false);
    private long _nextSeq = 0;
    private bool _shutdown = false;
    private bool _drain = false;

    public DelayScheduler(Func<long> clock = null) {
        if (clock != null) _clock = clock;
        else _clock = () => Environment.TickCount64;
        _worker = new Thread(WorkerLoop);
        _worker.IsBackground = true;          // never keeps the process alive on its own
        _worker.Start();
    }

    public Handle Schedule(Action work, TimeSpan delay) {
        if (work == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(work));
        long delayMs = (long)delay.TotalMilliseconds;
        if (delayMs < 0) delayMs = 0;

        lock (_lock) {
            if (_shutdown) throw new InvalidOperationException("scheduler is shut down");
            var handle = new Handle(this, work, _clock() + delayMs);
            _queue.Enqueue(handle, (handle.DueMs, _nextSeq));
            _nextSeq++;
            Monitor.PulseAll(_lock);          // the worker may be sleeping for a LATER task — wake it
            return handle;
        }
    }

    private bool TryCancel(Handle handle) {
        lock (_lock) {
            if (handle.Cancelled || handle.Started) return false;
            handle.Cancelled = true;          // stays in the heap; the worker throws it away later
            return true;
        }
    }

    private void WorkerLoop() {
        try {
            while (true) {
                Handle next = null;

                lock (_lock) {
                    while (next == null) {
                        // Stop: shut down and either not draining, or nothing left to drain.
                        if (_shutdown && (!_drain || _queue.Count == 0)) return;

                        if (_queue.Count == 0) {
                            Monitor.Wait(_lock);          // nothing to do: sleep until a pulse
                            continue;
                        }

                        Handle top = _queue.Peek();
                        if (top.Cancelled) {
                            _queue.Dequeue();             // drop cancelled tasks
                            continue;
                        }

                        long waitMs = top.DueMs - _clock();
                        if (waitMs <= 0) {
                            _queue.Dequeue();             // due: take it, run it below
                            top.Started = true;
                            next = top;
                        } else {
                            // Sleep until it is due. Wait releases the lock, and a pulse
                            // (new task, shutdown) wakes us early so we look again.
                            Monitor.Wait(_lock, (int)Math.Min(waitMs, int.MaxValue));
                        }
                    }
                }

                // Run OUTSIDE the lock: a slow task must not block Schedule or Cancel.
                try {
                    next.Work();
                } catch (Exception) {
                    // A failing task must not kill the worker. In production: log it.
                }
            }
        } finally {
            _terminated.Set();
        }
    }

    // Stop accepting work. drainPending = true runs what is already scheduled (at its due time).
    public void Shutdown(bool drainPending = true) {
        lock (_lock) {
            _shutdown = true;
            _drain = drainPending;
            Monitor.PulseAll(_lock);
        }
    }

    public bool AwaitTermination(TimeSpan timeout) {
        return _terminated.Wait(timeout);
    }

    public void Dispose() {
        Shutdown(drainPending: false);
        AwaitTermination(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1));
    }
}
```

